n","DOI":"10.1021/ic5013796","Year":2014,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-011249","value":"3.1","numeric":3.1}},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-011250","Abbreviation_in_the_article":1,"Cell_line":"A549","Time(h)":72,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":0.62,"IC50_Dark_value":0.62,"IC50_Light(M*10^-6)":0.11,"IC50_Light_value":0.11,"Excitation_Wavelength(nm)":">400","Irradiation_Time(minutes)":0.5,"Irradiation_Power(W*m^-2)":2333,"Method":"Resazurin","DOI":"10.1021/ic5013796","Year":2014,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-011250","value":"3.4","numeric":3.4}},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-011251","Abbreviation_in_the_article":1,"Cell_line":"Jurkat","Time(h)":72,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":1.63,"IC50_Dark_value":1.63,"IC50_Light(M*10^-6)":0.075,"IC50_Light_value":0.075,"Excitation_Wavelength(nm)":">400","Irradiation_Time(minutes)":0.5,"Irradiation_Power(W*m^-2)":2333,"Method":"Resazurin","DOI":"10.1021/ic5013796","Year":2014,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-011251","value":"0.5","numeric":0.5}},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-018553","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"D3","Cell_line":"NCI-H358","Time(h)":96,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":1.7,"IC50_Dark_value":1.7,"Method":"MTT","DOI":"10.1002/cmdc.202300347","Year":2023,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-018554","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"D3","Cell_line":"MCF-7","Time(h)":96,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":1.5,"IC50_Dark_value":1.5,"Method":"MTT","DOI":"10.1002/cmdc.202300347","Year":2023,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-038847","Abbreviation_in_the_article":3,"Cell_line":"A549","Time(h)":90,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":"0.6 ± 1.1","IC50_Dark_value":0.6,"IC50_Light(M*10^-6)":"0.1 ± 1.0","IC50_Light_value":0.1,"Excitation_Wavelength(nm)":">400","Irradiation_Time(minutes)":5,"Method":"Resazurin","DOI":"10.1039/c5tb01613d","Year":2016,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-038847","value":"2.5 ± 0.6","numeric":2.5}},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-052555","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru-SR3#","Cell_line":"PANC-1","Time(h)":24,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":6.6,"IC50_Dark_value":6.6,"Method":"CCK-8","DOI":"10.7150/thno.34015","Year":2019,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-052556","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru-SR3#","Cell_line":"TE-1","Time(h)":24,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":12.8,"IC50_Dark_value":12.8,"Method":"CCK-8","DOI":"10.7150/thno.34015","Year":2019,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-052557","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru-SR3#","Cell_line":"NCI-H1299","Time(h)":24,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":5,"IC50_Dark_value":5,"Method":"CCK-8","DOI":"10.7150/thno.34015","Year":2019,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"},{"Measurement_ID":"MCTM-052558","Abbreviation_in_the_article":"Ru-SR3#","Cell_line":"HBE","Time(h)":24,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":69.8,"IC50_Dark_value":69.8,"Method":"CCK-8","DOI":"10.7150/thno.34015","Year":2019,"Counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","Type":"IC50"}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"(1Ph2+)","value":1.9,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"PBS","pH":7.4,"doi":"10.1002/cmdc.201700240","year":2017,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-000150","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The lipophilicity of the ruthenium(II) complexes was determined using the shake-flask technique37 with 1-octanol and PBS (150 mm sodium chloride, 10 mm sodium phosphate, pH 7.4). PBS buffer is generally considered a more appropriate model of blood serum than pure water, as it better approximates the ionic strength and pH of blood plasma. The solute (33.3×10-5 m) was dissolved first in octanol and PBS, then the two saturated phases were shaken for 30 min at room temperature, and then let to equilibrate for 24 h. After this period, the distribution of the complex in each solvent was measured using a Hewlett–Packard HP8453A spectrophotometer. The corresponding concentration of the solute in each solvent was determined using Beer's law from the absorbance of the measured spectra; the concentration was then used in calculating the partition coefficient. The partition coefficient (Po/w) is the ratio of the equilibrium concentration of the dissolved compound in two phases [Eq. 1]: logPo/w = log([solute]octanol / [solute]water). The logP of the ruthenium complexes was also obtained for a biphasic solution of DI water and 1-octanol using the same general procedure above."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"(1Ph2+)","value":1.4,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1002/cmdc.201700240","year":2017,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-000162","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The lipophilicity of the ruthenium(II) complexes was determined using the shake-flask technique37 with 1-octanol and PBS (150 mm sodium chloride, 10 mm sodium phosphate, pH 7.4). PBS buffer is generally considered a more appropriate model of blood serum than pure water, as it better approximates the ionic strength and pH of blood plasma. The solute (33.3×10-5 m) was dissolved first in octanol and PBS, then the two saturated phases were shaken for 30 min at room temperature, and then let to equilibrate for 24 h. After this period, the distribution of the complex in each solvent was measured using a Hewlett–Packard HP8453A spectrophotometer. The corresponding concentration of the solute in each solvent was determined using Beer's law from the absorbance of the measured spectra; the concentration was then used in calculating the partition coefficient. The partition coefficient (Po/w) is the ratio of the equilibrium concentration of the dissolved compound in two phases [Eq. 1]: logPo/w = log([solute]octanol / [solute]water). The logP of the ruthenium complexes was also obtained for a biphasic solution of DI water and 1-octanol using the same general procedure above."},{"abbreviation_in_the_article":3,"value":1.8,"value_relation":"=","std":0.02,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1039/c5tb01613d","year":2016,"counterion":"[Cl-].[Cl-]","record_id":"MLDB-005046","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"Log P values were measured in triplicate by shake flask method. Because the densities of diH2O and n-octanol are quite different, both solvents were saturated with the other prior to testing to prevent volume changes resulting from phase equilibration that would affect the accuracy of log P measurements. Prior to testing, 40 mL of n-octanol was mixed with 10 mL of diH2O for 24 hrs to produce a saturated n-octanol solution. Similarly, 40 mL of diH2O was mixed with 10 mL of n-octanol to generate a saturated diH2O solution. While protected from light, 1 and 2 were prepared at 200 uM in diH2O (3 was prepared at 100 uM due to lower solubility in diH2O), diluted 1:1 with n-octanol solution to a total final volume of 1 mL, shaken vigorously for 10 min, and the phases separated by centrifugation at 17,000 x g for 30 sec. The absorbance of both solutions were analyzed using a Cary 60 spectrometer, and log P calculated based on the absorbance values at the wavelength associated with the MLCT peak of each Ru complex."}]}